summary:
FAIL. Two new Medium findings, both mandatory violations (access control and separation of duties), each reproduced through the API on a disposable PostgreSQL:
- F-DG5-100: a second methodology admin who also holds TO can rewrite another admin's Studio draft, approve its configured review and publish it. The self-review check knows only the creator and the requester.
- F-DG5-101: an organization-scope intake actor can claim, as capture_intake output, a transformation they cannot read. Rule A then moves the intake's steps into it.
Every required check ran:
- typecheck, build, lint, openapi:lint (846 operations), no-cdn and format: exit 0;
- unit on Node 24 and Node 22: 2335 / 1689 / 259 + 2 skipped;
- integration with C.UTF-8: 312/312 files (3030 passed + 1 skipped);
- integration with the locale unset: every candidate file passed. The exit was 1 only because of my own deleted sweep file, and two clean re-runs died on the shared disk's ENOSPC; all of this is disclosed;
- historical validators DG4-DG1 and --register DG5: PASS;
- the P5 AUD and ADM-only sweep: PASS.
The D-169 disclosures and the environmental residuals are accepted with reasons. Product gates G1-G6 are business approvals inside the product and are not engineering gates; nothing here approves anything, and product G6 never implies DG7.
